# Target C-II — Folkman–Graham strictness globalizes the residual/near-contact fork

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Status:** proof-only source-level structural strengthening
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** canonical bounded-face convexity; exact inactive-chamber residual conservation; low-degree face staircase; canonical contact-degree bound; Folkman–Graham 1969 proof of Oler's packing inequality
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_global_low_degree_oler_matching.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The degree-5/6 residual theorem used Wegner equality because it was available in the repository. The low-degree staircase now forces linearly many faces of bounded degree throughout the entire Type-II range. A direct equality analysis of the Folkman–Graham proof of Oler's inequality removes the 5/6 restriction.

The result is a global Type-II normal form:

\[
\boxed{
\text{either }\limsup R_F/D>0,
\quad\text{or there is an endpoint-disjoint matching }M_D
\text{ with }|M_D|>0.00063D-o(D)
}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\max_{xy\in M_D}(|x-y|-1)\to0.
}
\]

Thus every full-core Type-II survivor has either linear fixed-boundary residual or a linear source matching of inactive constraints approaching unit activation. No upper restriction on `t=(q-k)/D` remains.

## 1. Chamber residual is exactly Oler slack

Let `P` be a bounded exceptional contact face of degree `d`. Canonical bounded-face convexity makes `P` a simple strictly convex unit-edge polygon. Let `z_P` be the number of assigned contact-isolated source points in its chamber. The chamber source set consists of the `d` boundary vertices together with these `z_P` assigned points, so

\[
N_P=d+z_P,
\qquad \operatorname{per}(P)=d.
\]

With `a_0=sqrt(3)/2`, exact chamber residual conservation gives

\[
\boxed{
r_P={A_P\over a_0}+{d\over2}+1-N_P.
}
\tag{GO.1}
\]

This is precisely the slack in Oler's planar packing inequality

\[
N_P\le {A_P\over a_0}+{\operatorname{per}(P)\over2}+1.
\]

Residual conservation gives

\[
\boxed{\sum_P r_P\le R_F,\qquad r_P\ge0.}
\tag{GO.2}
\]

## 2. Strict Oler inequality when every hidden pair is longer than one

Call a pair of chamber source points a **boundary pair** only when it is one of the `d` consecutive unit sides of `P`. Every other pair is a **hidden pair**.

### Lemma GO.1

If every hidden pair in a chamber has distance strictly greater than one, then

\[
r_P>0.
\]

### Proof

Triangulate the convex chamber using every chamber source point as a vertex. Among all such triangulations choose one with minimum total edge length, exactly as in Folkman–Graham's proof of Oler's inequality.

Every boundary edge has length one. Since `d>=5`, the triangulation has an internal edge; every internal edge joins a hidden pair and therefore has length greater than one. Let `sigma` be a longest triangulation edge. Then `sigma` is internal, has length `d_0>1`, and is incident to two triangles.

Folkman–Graham Case III considers the convex quadrilateral `Q` formed by those two triangles. Minimality under the diagonal flip and maximality of `sigma` imply

\[
\text{each diagonal of }Q\ge\text{each side of }Q\ge1.
\]

Their Lemma 2 proves

\[
\Phi(Q):={4\over\sqrt3}A(Q)-P(Q)+2\ge0.
\tag{GO.3}
\]

We only need to inspect equality in that elementary proof. Write the two angles opposite `sigma` as `theta,theta'`, with adjacent side pairs `(s,t)` and `(s',t')`. The proof gives

\[
A(Q)\ge{\sqrt3\over4}(st+s't')
\]

and hence

\[
\Phi(Q)\ge(s-1)(t-1)+(s'-1)(t'-1)\ge0.
\]

If equality held, then

\[
\sin\theta=\sin\theta'={\sqrt3\over2},
\]

and at least one side in each of `(s,t)` and `(s',t')` would equal one. Because `sigma` is at least as long as either adjacent side, each opposite angle is at least `pi/3`.

If `theta=pi/3`, the cosine law with one adjacent side equal to one and `d_0>=` the other adjacent side forces that other side also to equal one and then `d_0=1`. Since `d_0>1`, equality would instead force `theta=2pi/3`. The same argument forces `theta'=2pi/3`. But Folkman–Graham choose this opposite pair with

\[
\theta+\theta'\le\pi,
\]

contradiction. Therefore

\[
\boxed{\Phi(Q)>0.}
\tag{GO.4}
\]

The Case-III induction inserts one half of `Phi(Q)` into the Oler functional. Hence the final Oler inequality for the chamber is strict, proving `r_P>0`.

## 3. Uniform stability for every fixed degree cutoff

Fix an integer `r>=5` and `eta>0`. Consider chambers of degree `5<=d<=r` in which every hidden pair has distance at least `1+eta`.

For fixed `d`, the convex unit-edge polygon has bounded diameter, so unit separation gives a finite upper bound on the number `z_P` of assigned interior points. Hence only finitely many pairs `(d,z_P)` occur. Modulo rigid motion, each corresponding configuration family is compact.

Residual `r_P` is continuous. Lemma GO.1 excludes zero residual on every one of these compact families. Therefore there is a uniform constant

\[
\boxed{c_r(eta)>0}
\tag{GO.5}
\]

such that every degree-`<=r` chamber with all hidden pairs at least `1+eta` satisfies

\[
r_P\ge c_r(eta).
\]

This is the all-fixed-degree replacement for the earlier degree-5/6 Wegner stability lemma.

## 4. Insert the universal degree-22 face floor

The modern low-degree staircase proves throughout every Type-II sequence

\[
\boxed{F_{\le22}>0.0057D-o(D).}
\tag{GO.6}
\]

Suppose now that `R_F=o(D)`. Fix `eta>0`. By (GO.2) and (GO.5),

\[
\#\{P:\deg P\le22,\ h(P)\ge eta\}
\le {R_F\over c_{22}(eta)}=o(D),
\tag{GO.7}
\]

where `h(P)` is the minimum hidden-pair gap `|x-y|-1` in the chamber. Thus all but `o(D)` of the faces in (GO.6) contain a hidden pair shorter than `1+eta`.

A diagonal choice `eta_D downarrow0` gives at least

\[
(0.0057-o(1))D
\]

bounded degree-`<=22` chambers, each with one selected inactive pair of length `1+o(1)`, uniformly over the selected family.

## 5. Endpoint-disjoint extraction costs only a factor nine

Choose one such hidden pair from each selected chamber. A boundary contact vertex belongs to at most `deg_C(v)<=5` bounded face boundaries. An assigned contact-isolated point belongs to only its assigned chamber. Therefore a source point is an endpoint of at most five selected pair occurrences.

The selected pairs form a multigraph of maximum degree at most five. One selected pair conflicts with at most eight other occurrences, so greedy edge selection gives an endpoint-disjoint matching of at least one ninth of all selected pairs.

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
|M_D|>{0.0057\over9}D-o(D)>0.00063D-o(D),
}
\tag{GO.8}

and every pair in `M_D` is inactive with

\[
\boxed{
1<|x-y|=1+o(1)
}
\tag{GO.9}

uniformly on the matching.

## 6. Scope and remaining obstruction

This theorem uses only fixed-degree compactness; it does not assert an explicit modulus `c_22(eta)`. It also does not claim simultaneous activation of the matching.

The high-t residual route is no longer limited by the 5/6 Wegner equality classification or by the scalar `t` endpoint. The remaining fork is global:

```text
linear fixed-boundary residual R_F
or
linear endpoint-disjoint almost-active source matching.
```

Closing C-II now requires either an upper/payment contradiction for linear `R_F`, or an activation/finite-exchange theorem for the almost-active matching in the lexicographically normalized diameter minimizer.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only global residual/near-contact matching fork for the entire Type-II range. C-II and Level I remain open.
